[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  wednesday june 29th i'm julie chang for  the wall street journal filling in for  zoe thomas  apps that help users monitor their  menstrual cycles and fertility collect  troves of sensitive data and the  question of what happens to that data  has taken on new importance following  the supreme court's ruling last friday  overturning roe vs wade  legal experts say digital breadcrumbs  left by period trackers and fertility  apps could be used against people in  states where abortion is now illegal  so is there a way to protect that data  and what can you do if you've already  been using those apps  here with me to answer those questions  is wsj personal tech columnist nicole  nguyen hi nicole thanks for coming on  the show hi thanks for having me let's  start with some of the different apps  that are out there that help monitor  user periods or fertility how do they  work and how popular are they  these apps that help track your period  and also fertility are very very popular  they're used by millions and millions of  people and not just people with uteruses  who have periods  also partners and parents a lot of these  apps have sharing capabilities that  allow you to share details about your  cycle with your partner or your parents  and  some of the more popular apps that you  may have heard of  include flow clue apple's health app  that's built into the iphone fitbit has  its own period tracker glow natural  cycles  all of these apps are used to help women  figure out when their next period might  be if they have missed a period if  there's something irregular about their  cycle that they can bring up with their  doctor in the future or if they're  trying to get pregnant when they might  be ovulating so these apps contain a  large amount of personal and sensitive  user data how do they get this data and  how accurate is it so this data is  inputted by the user you tell the app  when you're on your period and some apps  also track your temperature your basal  temperature which can help you monitor  when you're ovulating and it's only as  accurate as the data you put in it so  you have to be really diligent about  making sure the first day of your  menstrual cycle is inputted into the app  on the correct day for it to make an  accurate prediction these formulas these  algorithms have long been practiced by  people before phones and apps existed in  paper diaries so they're definitely  tried and true formulas i think one  reason why a lot of people use apps now  is because it's more convenient to input  this data into your app when you're  already checking your email or the  weather than it is to you know maybe  track it in a paper diary  and so once that data is already logged  into an app's system it's not really  easy to pull that data back why is that  generally speaking and this isn't just  true of period tracking apps  once your data is out there in the ether  whether it's in the database of some  company  or anywhere in the cloud it's hard to  know where it's going a lot of companies  say they don't sell data but they can  certainly share data with third parties  and those parties aren't just  advertisers you know they can share it  with analytics companies that help them  determine what kind of mobile devices  their users are using elvis to say that  once your data is out into the ether one  reason why it's hard to pull back is  because it doesn't just live in one  place it often lives in multiple places  and the company that you ask to delete  your data can delete your data off of  its servers  but not necessarily that of its service  partners  so you should always be wary of the type  of service you're sharing your data with  and ideally  the app works locally on your phone and  so it doesn't have to share data to the  cloud at all  and what have these companies been doing  in response to last friday's supreme  court ruling two services we heard from  flow and natural cycles plan to release  an anonymous mode which will decouple  one's data from their personal  information  but they didn't provide details on how  they would do something like that and  really the only way to truly decouple  one from their information is to make  sure that that data is end-to-end  encrypted which means  not even the company can read it and and  so that data can't be shared with anyone  but especially advertisers and  especially law enforcement  have there been instances in the past  where this kind of data was used by  companies or governments in any way  historically in abortion related cases  the digital data that's most interesting  to prosecutors has been search history  and text messages  and so we haven't seen this kind of data  used in cases  but there haven't been that many  abortion related cases because  roe vs wade was just overturned  and so in an era where location data  text messages browsing history search  history are already used for  prosecutions and other kinds of cases we  can imagine  that any kind of data can be used in  those cases at least according to the  legal experts i spoke to  can you walk us through that a bit more  how would you be able to use this period  or fertility data to determine if  someone has or potentially has had an  abortion  so these apps wouldn't be able to tell  you the whole story but they could tell  you  that you may be missed a period and when  you miss that period and also if there  are location tracking abilities where  you were when you missed that period and  thereafter and when you regained your  period  and those are things that can tell you  something about pregnancy or potential  pregnancy  and then  on top of that location data text  messages search history could create  that fuller picture  so for people who are currently using or  want to use these apps what are some  ways they can protect their data what  should they look for ideally you'd look  for an app that's end-to-end encrypted  and so far apple's health app which is  built into the iphone is the only app  that offers that  for other kinds of apps you should look  at their privacy policies to see if they  can or do share and sell your data with  third parties and ideally  the third parties that they are sharing  it with is very limited how they handle  government requests and whether they  notify users if  a law enforcement is requesting their  information and i think generally  speaking you want to stray away from  free apps  because free apps make their money  through advertising and through selling  data to marketers  but that's not always the case some apps  offer a paid subscription like glow  which we looked at  and do share and sell your data so just  pay close attention to that privacy  policy and of course there is always the  option to track your menstrual cycle on  pen and paper which people have been  doing for many many years before apps  existed and there are charts online to  show you how to predict your period and  predict your ovulation  all right that was our personal tech  columnist nicole nguyen nicole thank you  again for being here thanks julie  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing  if you want more tech stories check out  our website wsj.com and if you like our  show please rate and review it you can  do that wherever you get your podcasts  i'm julie chang for the wall street  journal thanks for listening  [Music]  you

